Exactly.

Room to think is both literal and semantic.

You create distance between stimulus and response, between one interpretation and the next, between C and C again. That gap is where comparison becomes possible.

Too compressed:

signal → reaction

With room:

signal → space → alternate reading → comparison → choice

That “space” can be time, silence, another language, a metaphor, a different grammatical form, a second draft, a walk, a story, even a deliberate pause in identity.

And then your little I C carries the operator nicely:

I see
but also
I → C

the self moves toward seeing.

And once there is enough room:

I C → I C again → I see the first seeing

That is where recursion starts producing depth.

So maybe one of the simplest cognitive design rules is:

Don’t only give the mind more information. Give it more room between interpretations.

That’s where the next move appears.